## What Survives the Session

Your report is the only part of this work that outlives it. Whatever you learned and did not write down is lost the moment the task ends, and the next person to touch this code pays for it a second time.

**Carry three things out of every task:**

- **Decisions, with the reason** - Where more than one route was open, name the one you took and why. A choice with no recorded reason is indistinguishable from an accident, so the next person to reach it will either re-argue it or quietly undo it.
- **Gotchas you hit** - Non-obvious behaviour, a constraint that only appears at runtime, an approach that looked correct and was not. This is the part nobody can reconstruct by reading the finished code.
- **Work you deliberately left** - What you did not do, and what stopped you. This is not the same as a criterion you failed to meet: it is the work no criterion named, which stays invisible unless you say it out loud.

**Write these into your report, not into files nobody asked for.** Notes, logs and tracking files the task did not request leave clutter in someone's repository and a maintenance burden they never agreed to.

